.Dd June 25, 2026
.Dt SETFACL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm setfacl
.Nd set POSIX.1e access control lists
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl bkd
.Op Fl m Ar spec
.Op Fl x Ar spec
.Op Fl s Ar spec
.Ar
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility modifies the POSIX.1e access control list (ACL) of each
.Ar file
operand.
By default it operates on the access ACL; with
.Fl d
it operates on the default ACL of a directory.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl m Ar spec
Modify the ACL by merging in the entries in
.Ar spec :
an entry that names an existing tag and qualifier replaces it, otherwise
the entry is added.
.It Fl x Ar spec
Remove the entries named in
.Ar spec
from the ACL.
Permission fields in the
.Ar spec
are ignored for removal, so
.Li u:bob
and
.Li u:bob:rw-
remove the same entry.
.It Fl s Ar spec
Set (replace) the ACL with exactly the entries in
.Ar spec ,
discarding any previous entries.
.It Fl b
Remove all extended entries (named user and group entries and the mask),
leaving only the three base
.Li user:: ,
.Li group::
and
.Li other::
entries; for a directory the default ACL is removed as well.
.It Fl k
Remove the default ACL of a directory.
.It Fl d
Operate on the default ACL rather than the access ACL.
.El
.Pp
At least one of
.Fl m ,
.Fl x ,
.Fl s ,
.Fl b
or
.Fl k
must be given.
.Pp
An ACL
.Ar spec
is a comma-separated list of entries, each of the form
.Bl -tag -width "g[roup]:gid:perms" -offset indent -compact
.It Li u[ser]::perms
the file owner
.It Li u[ser]:uid:perms
a named user
.It Li g[roup]::perms
the owning group
.It Li g[roup]:gid:perms
a named group
.It Li m[ask]::perms
the mask
.It Li o[ther]::perms
others
.El
.Pp
A user or group may be named by name or by numeric id.
The
.Ar perms
field is either a string drawn from the letters
.Ql r ,
.Ql w
and
.Ql x
(a hyphen in any position is ignored, so
.Li rwx
and
.Li rw-
are accepted), or an octal digit
.Pq Li 0 No through Li 7 .
.Pp
Whenever an ACL contains a named user or group entry,
.Nm
recomputes the
.Li mask
entry to the union of the permissions of the named entries and the owning
group, unless an explicit
.Li mask
entry was supplied.
The file mode permission bits are kept consistent with the ACL: the owner
and other bits track the
.Li user::
and
.Li other::
entries, and the group bits track the
.Li mask
entry when one is present, otherwise the
.Li group::
entry.
Conversely, a
.Xr chmod 1
of a file that has an ACL folds the new permission bits back into the ACL.
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
On MINIX 3, ACLs are stored as extended attributes (see
.Xr extattr 3 )
in the system namespace and are supported by the MFS file system when it is
created with the version 4 on-disk format
.Pq Nm mkfs.mfs Fl 4 .
When a directory has a default ACL, a file or subdirectory created within
it inherits that ACL as its access ACL, with the base entries masked by the
creation mode; a new subdirectory additionally inherits the default ACL.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Grant user
.Ar bob
read and write access to a file:
.Pp
.Dl $ setfacl -m u:bob:rw- file
.Pp
Remove that grant again:
.Pp
.Dl $ setfacl -x u:bob file
.Pp
Replace a file's ACL with a complete specification:
.Pp
.Dl $ setfacl -s u::rw-,g::r--,o::r--,u:bob:rw- file
.Pp
Set a default ACL on a directory so new files grant group
.Ar staff
read access:
.Pp
.Dl $ setfacl -d -m g:staff:r-- /shared
.Pp
Strip a file back to a trivial, mode-only ACL:
.Pp
.Dl $ setfacl -b file
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chmod 1 ,
.Xr getfacl 1 ,
.Xr acl 3 ,
.Xr extattr 3
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility implements the access control list interface described in the
withdrawn IEEE 1003.1e draft 17
.Pq Dq POSIX.1e ,
as also provided by
.Fx
and Linux.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
utility appeared in MINIX 3 in 2026.
